      <div class="faq-main">

        <h2 id="faq-heading" class="section-heading fade-up">About Dental Implants</h2>

        <div class="faq-list" role="list">

          <div class="faq-item fade-up" role="listitem">
            <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-1">
              Am I a candidate for dental implants?
            </button>
            <div id="faq-1" class="faq-answer">
              <p>Most adults in good general health are candidates for dental implants. Adequate jawbone volume is important, but bone grafting can address deficiencies in many cases. A consultation including 3D imaging with Dr. Pasicznyk will provide a definitive answer for your situation.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item fade-up" role="listitem">
            <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">
              How long does the full implant process take?
            </button>
            <div id="faq-2" class="faq-answer">
              <p>For single implants, the process from placement to final crown typically takes three to six months. Full-arch cases can involve a same-day provisional set of teeth, with final restorations placed after osseointegration is complete — usually several months later. Every case is different, and your timeline will be outlined during the planning process.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item fade-up" role="listitem">
            <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">
              Is the surgery painful?
            </button>
            <div id="faq-3" class="faq-answer">
              <p>You should not feel pain during surgery. Every procedure uses local anesthesia, and for longer or more complex cases a board-certified MD anesthesiologist provides IV sedation by a board-certified MD anesthesiologist, so many patients are fully relaxed throughout. Some soreness afterward is normal and is usually managed with over-the-counter medication. Dr. Pasicznyk will go over your recovery plan before surgery day.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item fade-up" role="listitem">
            <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">
              What if I've been told I don't have enough bone?
            </button>
            <div id="faq-4" class="faq-answer">
              <p>Insufficient bone doesn't necessarily disqualify you. <a href="/bone-grafting">Bone grafting</a> can rebuild volume in many cases. For patients with severe upper-jaw bone loss, <a href="/zygomatic-implants">zygomatic implants</a> anchored in the cheekbone may be an option. A thorough consultation and imaging study will tell us what's possible.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item fade-up" role="listitem">
            <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">
              Do you offer sedation?
            </button>
            <div id="faq-5" class="faq-answer">
              <p>Yes. Foundation Dental Center offers local anesthesia, nitrous oxide, and IV sedation. IV sedation is provided by a board-certified MD anesthesiologist, who stays with you for the entire procedure and focuses only on your comfort and safety. You will need someone to drive you home after IV sedation. <a href="/anesthesia">Learn more about comfort and anesthesia options.</a></p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item fade-up" role="listitem">
            <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-6">
              How do I care for my implants?
            </button>
            <div id="faq-6" class="faq-answer">
              <p>Dental implants are cared for much like natural teeth — regular brushing, flossing (or interdental cleaning), and professional check-ups. Implants do not decay, but the surrounding tissue can develop problems if oral hygiene is neglected. Your post-treatment instructions will cover the specifics for your restoration type.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item fade-up" role="listitem">
            <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-7">
              What is the difference between a removable implant denture and a fixed one?
            </button>
            <div id="faq-7" class="faq-answer">
              <p>A removable implant overdenture snaps onto implants and can be taken out by the patient for cleaning. A fixed hybrid prosthesis is screwed in and only removable by the provider. Fixed restorations are generally preferred for their stability and feel, but both offer significant advantages over conventional dentures. <a href="/implant-dentures">Learn more about implant dentures.</a></p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item fade-up" role="listitem">
            <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-8">
              What are your hours?
            </button>
